# Experiment configs are hashed byte-for-byte by checkpoint provenance.
# Pin checkout EOL so their SHA-256 values do not depend on core.autocrlf.
#
# Both files below are canonically LF. Their SHA-256 values are recorded
# inside the trained checkpoints and the run summaries, and the evaluation
# gate in src/ct_restoration/evaluation_integrity.py re-hashes the working
# file and refuses to score a checkpoint whose recorded hash no longer
# matches. Without these rules a CRLF checkout would fail provenance on a
# scientifically identical config.
#
# Deliberately narrow: only raw-byte-hashed experiment configs are pinned.
# The run summaries and training histories are read as JSON/CSV, never
# re-hashed, so their line endings cannot affect any gate. The checkpoints
# are hashed but are not tracked by Git.
configs/cnn.yaml   text eol=lf
configs/unet.yaml  text eol=lf

# Milestone 10 will freeze one config per additional statistical seed under
# configs/multiseed/. Each is byte-hashed by its run's checkpoint provenance
# exactly as the two above are, so the rule is added now: a per-seed config
# is pinned from the moment it is created, not after a CRLF checkout has
# already made a run unverifiable on someone else's machine.
configs/multiseed/*.yaml  text eol=lf

# The frozen patient split and slice manifest. The split's SHA-256 is published
# in the README and asserted byte for byte by tests/test_splits.py, and a CRLF
# checkout under core.autocrlf=true changes those bytes without changing a
# single row - so the guard on the frozen split fails on exactly the platform
# this project runs on. The committed bytes are already LF, so pinning them
# changes no hash; it only stops checkout from rewriting them.
data/splits/*.csv  text eol=lf
